<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ecuador</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Ecuador</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ecuador is a country in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, and includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands">Galápagos Islands</a> about 1,000 kilometers west of the mainland. Several indigenous groups lived on its land and were gradually incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> during the 15th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> colonised the territory during the 16th century. Ecuador achieved independence in 1820 as part of Gran Colombia and emerged as a sovereign state in 1830. The legacy of both empires is reflected in its ethnically diverse population, and 13 native languages, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and Shuar, are recognised beside Spanish.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecuador.jpg" alt="Ecuador" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: President of the Republic of Ecuador, first uploaded by Denelson83 as Flag of Ecuador.svg, modifications by Husunqu, Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ecuador.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological evidence suggests that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleo-indians">Paleo-Indians</a> first dispersed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> near the end of the last glacial period, around 16,500 to 13,000 years ago, and the first people to reach Ecuador may have come by land from North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> or by boat down the Pacific coast. In the first decades of Spanish rule new diseases such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> caused high mortality among the Amerindian population, who were also forced into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> labour system. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> became the seat of a Spanish audiencia in 1563, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riobamba">Riobamba</a> earthquake of 1797, which caused up to 40,000 casualties, was studied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a> when he visited in 1801–1802.</p><p>On 9 October 1820 the Department of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> became the first territory in Ecuador to gain independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The rest followed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a> defeated the Spanish Royalist forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pichincha">Battle of Pichincha</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, and Ecuador joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s Gran Colombia, having been claimed also by the Peruvian liberation army of San Martín. The name comes from the Ecuador Department of Gran Colombia, established in 1824. Ecuador separated on 13 May 1830, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-jose-flores">Juan José Flores</a> as its first president, and annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands">Galápagos Islands</a> two years later. In 1840 Flores signed a treaty in which Spain recognised Ecuadorian independence.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1895">Liberal Revolution of 1895</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eloy-alfaro">Eloy Alfaro</a> reduced the power of the clergy and the conservative landowners, and liberal governments held power until the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-revolution">Julian Revolution</a> of 1925. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-velasco-ibarra">José María Velasco Ibarra</a> in 1972, and military rule continued until elections on 29 April 1979 under a new constitution. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaime-roldos-aguilera">Jaime Roldós Aguilera</a> took office on 10 August as the first constitutionally elected president after nearly a decade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorships</a>, and died in an air crash on 24 May 1981.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ecuador lies on the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands">Galápagos Islands</a>, about 1,000 kilometres from the mainland. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> is the capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> the largest city. A 2021 estimate put the population at 17,797,737, about 71 per cent of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a>. Spanish is the official language, spoken by most people, and 13 native languages are recognised, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> and Shuar. The constitution of 2008, approved by referendum, was the first to recognise legally enforceable rights of nature. The country is divided into 24 provinces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ecuador” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2001 archaeologists found twenty decorated trumpets made from conch shells, brought from the seas off <b>Ecuador</b>, hidden in a small gallery beside the temple’s Circular Plaza.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chavin">Chavín · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The memory of the union survives in symbols: the national flags of Colombia, Venezuela and <b>Ecuador</b> all descend from the yellow, blue and red tricolour of Francisco de Miranda adopted by the republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Túpac Inca Yupanqui, first as general and from about 1471 as ruler, conquered the Kingdom of Chimor on the north coast and pushed the frontiers into <b>Ecuador</b> and south into Chile, while Huayna Capac, from about 1493, extended rule to the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Andrés Bello, a Venezuelan scholar who settled in Chile, wrote its Civil Code of 1855, which was later adopted by <b>Ecuador</b> and Colombia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ecuador</b>’s 2008 constitution was the first in the world to give nature itself enforceable rights, allowing any person to go to court on behalf of a river or forest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the twentieth century Peru fought Colombia over Leticia in 1932–1933 and <b>Ecuador</b> in 1941, 1981 and 1995, and from 1980 to 2000 the armed forces waged a brutal counter-insurgency against the Shining Path.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Military</a></li></ul></section>
